<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Welsh people</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Welsh are the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, a Celtic nation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, whose name for themselves, Cymry, comes from a Brittonic word meaning &quot;fellow countrymen&quot;. They descend from the Britons who kept their language after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> settlements, and Welsh is today the most widely spoken Celtic language. Early modern antiquarians wrongly linked the name Cymry with the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimmerians">Cimmerians</a>, a theory long rejected.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/welsh-people.jpg" alt="Welsh people" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown Vector graphics by Tobias Jakobs · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Wales.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The English words <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> and Welsh descend from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> wealh, itself from a Proto-Germanic word derived from the name of the Gaulish people the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volcae">Volcae</a>. It first meant inhabitants of the Roman Empire in general, then the Britons in particular, and as their territories shrank it came to name the area that best kept continuity with pre-Anglo-Saxon <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. The Romans met tribes in what is now Wales that they called the Ordovices, the Demetae, the Silures and the Deceangli. All the peoples of southern Britain were Britons who spoke Common Brittonic, a Celtic language that seems to have arrived in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>, though some archaeologists see no evidence of large-scale migration and favour cultural diffusion.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-legion">Roman legions</a> left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> around 400, a Romano-British culture remained where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> had settled. The people of what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> kept speaking Common Brittonic with much <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> influence, and it evolved into Old Welsh. Offa's Dyke, raised in the mid-8th century, formed a barrier between Wales and Mercia. How the native population came to think of itself as Welsh is not clear. The Welsh word Cymry, from a Brythonic word meaning fellow-countrymen, drew on the relationship with the Brittonic peoples of the Old North in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>. Probably in use before the 7th century, it is attested in a praise poem to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cadwallon-ap-cadfan">Cadwallon ap Cadfan</a> around 633, and after about 1100 it prevailed over the older Brythoniaid.</p><p>Immigration followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norman-conquest">Norman Conquest</a>, and the Landsker Line dividing the Pembrokeshire Englishry from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/welshry">Welshry</a> can still be traced. The population of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> more than doubled from 587,000 in 1801 to 1,163,000 in 1851 and reached 2,421,000 by 1911, mostly in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a>-mining districts. English, Irish and Italian migrants came during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/industrial-revolution">Industrial Revolution</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Before the 20th century many Welsh people spoke only Welsh. Parts of the culture are closely tied to the language, notably the Eisteddfod tradition, poetry and aspects of folk music and dance. Forms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> have dominated religious life for more than 1,400 years, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> has a long tradition of nonconformism and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/methodism">Methodism</a>. The Sunday Closing (Wales) Act of 1881 was the first legislation issued specifically for Wales since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. The flag shows the red dragon, which Henry VII carried at Bosworth in 1485 and which is recorded as a Welsh symbol in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/historia-brittonum">Historia Brittonum</a> around 820. Welsh emigrants settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patagonia">Patagonia</a>, where the settlement is called Y Wladfa.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Welsh people” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their name survived in Greek legend, in the Cimmerian Bosporus and, through Welsh antiquarians, in the mistaken idea that they were ancestors of the <b>Cymry</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/cimmerians">Cimmerians</a></li></ul></section>
